            <ArticleTitle>Social problem solving in chronic pain: An integrative model of coping predicts mental health in chronic pain patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1015-25</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1359105314544133</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Despite several models of coping have been proposed in chronic pain, research is not integrative and has not yet identified a reliable set of beneficial coping strategies. We intend to offer a comprehensive view of coping using the social problem-solving model. Participants were 369 chronic pain patients (63.78% women; mean age 58.89 years; standard deviation = 15.12 years). Correlation analyses and the structural equation model for mental health revealed potentially beneficial and harmful problem-solving components. This integrative perspective on general coping could be used to promote changes in the way patients deal with stressful conditions other than pain.</AbstractText>
